// An index expression addresses an ELEMENT of a container.
//
// A scalar (an int, a bool, a char, ...) has no elements at all: the
// index was lowered as an offset from a number, so `x[0]` read or wrote
// through a wild address -- the program died with a segmentation fault
// (exit -11) instead of being refused.
//
// The receiver's type counts as proven only where the checker knows it: a
// local, a parameter, a class field, a program-level binding, or a
// literal. Everything else (a member read, a call result, an untyped
// `var`) stays permissive, because an unknown shape is not a proven
// scalar.
void main() {
    int x = 5;
    int c = x[0];
    printline(c);
}
// expect-diagnostic: expected 'an indexable value'
